		was set to be skipped: 10px mono, letter-spaced, in the palette's dimmest
		grey. Three separate things all pulling legibility down at once, on the
		sentence that makes the section trustworthy.

		Now it is a note that can be read. Body face, one step up in size, at
		bone-100; the letter-spacing is gone, because tracking added to small text
		is what made it a smear rather than a line. The accent rule down the side
		does the job the mono and the grey were doing — marking it as apparatus
		rather than argument — without costing anything to read.
	*/
	.exhibit__source {
		margin-block-start: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
		max-width: 68ch;
		padding-inline-start: 0.875rem;
		border-inline-start: 2px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-arc-400) 55%, transparent);
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		line-height: 1.65;
